(mental-model, rationale) topics keep a USER_GUIDE.md derivation hash — each records the section anchor (a stable HTML-comment id, NOT heading text, so a heading reword does not red the build for a non-reason) plus a SHA-256 of the bytes between the markers, and `HelpTopicCorpusTest.topicDerivationHashesMatchCurrentUserGuide` reds the build when the guide drifts under a topic; (b) code-fact topics pin the FACT to the runtime source, not the guide — `HelpTopicCorpusTest.forgetErasureTopicMatchesPurgeService` asserts the `/forget` topic enumerates exactly the categories `ForgetPurgeService.PurgeResult`'s record components derive (chat_memory, chat_session, summary_anchor personal-only, saved_post global-per-D13), and `HelpTopicCorpusTest.probationTopicDurationMatchesApplicationConfig` asserts the probation topic's stated duration matches `infochat.probation.duration` from the main `application.properties`. The guide hash alone detects guide CHANGE, not guide/code MISMATCH, so it would not have caught either USER_GUIDE defect M1-649 also fixes; the code-fact pin catches the drift the guide hash CANNOT — code changing while the guide (and the topic) stay stale-green. **Tier-flat by construction:** every topic is user-tier; `HelpTopicCorpusTest.noTopicReferencesAdminSurface` asserts no topic (match text OR answer value, EN or CS) names a `HelpCommandHandler.HelpTier.BOT_ADMIN` command. The pin is against the bot-admin surface, NOT against `LlmOutputSanitizer.CLOSED_LIST` membership — the group-admin commands topics must name (`/add-source`, `/lang`, `/follow-tag`, …) are themselves `CLOSED_LIST` entries and are expected in topic text. This is necessary but NOT sufficient for delivery safety: the user-reachable commands topics must name are in the sanitizer `CLOSED_LIST`, which is exactly why M1-666 delivers deterministically post-sanitize rather than through the model. **doc_id namespace disjointness:** every topic `doc_id` is namespaced (`topic:<slug>`); V60's PRIMARY KEY is single-column on `doc_id` and the upsert DELETE is `doc_kind`-scoped, so a topic doc_id colliding with a command name would miss the command row on DELETE and PK-violate on INSERT — rolling back the batch and silently degrading the corpus. Namespacing plus `HelpTopicCorpusTest.topicDocIdsDisjointFromCommandNames` forecloses it without a schema migration. **This row records the corpus's existence and shape; it is explicitly silent on chat delivery** — topic delivery is M1-666. Companion to D66 (the command corpus this sits alongside) and D67 (the deterministic delivery path M1-666 will reuse for topics). Recorded by M1-649. |
 
