 reply.digest.closing_affordance=@mention me to go deeper on any story, or ask about a topic you don't see here.
 
+# ----- Conceptual topic corpus (M1-649, D68) -------------------------------
+# Per docs/spec/commands.md §Chat mode + decision D68. The corpus lives in
+# HelpTopicCorpus (one intent-shaped match doc per topic, indexed under
+# doc_kind='topic'); these keys are the SERVED answers — reviewed product
+# copy, plain text (D30), bundle-localized (D43), bypassing TranslationPipeline
+# (the D43 two-path rule, same as D67's deterministic usage block). Naming:
+# topic.<slug>.answer mirrors the topic's HelpTopicCorpus slug. Delivery is
+# M1-666; M1-649 ships retrieval only.
+topic.getting-access.answer=infochat is invite-only. An admin gives you a one-time invite code; send that code to the bot as your first direct message and you'll be registered and welcomed. New accounts start in a short probation period with a limited command set and chat mode turned off — this is normal and temporary. Full access unlocks when probation ends, or sooner if an admin vouches for you. Run /help at any time to see exactly what you can do right now.
+topic.probation.answer=Probation is a slow-start phase every new account goes through. For the first 24h after you register (per the deployment's infochat.probation.duration setting) you get a limited command set and chat mode is turned off — only read-only catalogue commands plus /lang, /forget, and /stop work. Once probation ends you get the full command set and chat mode unlocks. An admin can also end probation early by vouching for you. The welcome message and /help show the probation window and exactly which commands are available.
+topic.chat-vs-commands.answer=You can talk to the bot two ways. Commands start with a slash — like /summary, /save, /follow-tag — and perform exact, repeatable actions. Anything else is a chat question in plain language, good for follow-ups and exploring. You can mix both freely in the same conversation. The bot replies in plain text; any links appear as bare web addresses you can tap or copy.
+topic.chat-assistant-boundary.answer=The chat assistant is read-only. It searches and explains your news; it can never change settings, post anything, or act as an admin. It only sees your own subscriptions and the scope you're chatting in — it cannot reach another user's data or another conversation. If a reply is taking too long, /stop cancels it.
+topic.dm-vs-group.answer=infochat works in both DMs and groups. In a DM, just message the bot to get a reply. In a group, @mention the bot — or wait for its scheduled morning and evening digests. Group-wide settings (language, digest times, the group's sources) are managed by group admins, but your personal context and saves are always yours and never shared with other group members. Approved groups also get automatic digests of the latest posts.
+topic.unfollow-vs-delete.answer=/unfollow-source <id> only stops the source from appearing in your scope (just you in a DM, or the whole group when a group admin acts there). The source itself stays — other scopes still see it, and its posts keep arriving for them. Deleting a source entirely (removing it from the deployment) is a bot-admin action — ask an admin if a shared source should go. So: unfollow is per-scope and reversible; delete is global, permanent, and admin-only.
+topic.add-source-requires-tags.answer=/add-source requires at least one --tags value because every post must be sortable by topic, and automatic tagging can miss. By attaching at least one tag at source-add time you guarantee the source's posts can still be routed into digests and /summary even when the auto-tagger has nothing confident to say. Tags set at source-add time also become the source's shared bootstrap tags — visible to everyone who follows that source — so pick them with that in mind. Use /follow-tag (your own view) to tune what you personally see.
+topic.personal-vs-shared-tags.answer=There are two layers of tags. Each source carries shared bootstrap tags, set when the source was added and changeable only by a bot admin — these affect everyone who follows that source. On top of that, each user has personal /follow-tag selections that tune only their own view: by default you see everything from your sources, and the moment you /follow-tag a topic you switch to "only the topics I picked" (just for you, in this scope). /unfollow-tag drops one topic; /unfollow-tag --all resets you to the default everything-from-my-sources view.
+topic.clear-vs-forget.answer=/clear and /forget both wipe conversation data, but at different scopes. /clear drops only the short-term context of the current conversation — your longer-term memory and saved posts stay. /forget is the full privacy purge: it wipes everything the bot keeps on your behalf in this scope (chat memory, the current conversation, and your saved-posts library — saves are global, so they get wiped no matter which conversation you run /forget from). /clear is reversible: the bot rebuilds context as you chat. /forget is irreversible and asks you to confirm first.
+topic.forget-erasure.answer=/forget erases, for the scope you run it in: your chat memory (chat_memory rows for this conversation), your chat sessions and their messages (chat_session and chat_message rows for this conversation), and your personal summary anchors (summary_anchor rows marked personal for this conversation). It also wipes your saved-posts library globally — saved_post rows for your user, across every scope, because saves are global per decision D13. /forget does NOT touch: your ban or admin status, your group memberships, or the system's tamper-proof audit log (which records that actions happened, never your message content). It tells you if you still have data in other conversations so you can clear those too.
+
